60,3,1,talk,Asuka,,"Candidate A, Home Follow-Up, aligns with the readmission success. But the tariff issue is real.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
61,3,1,talk,Toshio,,"Candidate C, Day Rehab, needs dedicated space. The booking system can handle fixed slots, but space is tight.","{""words"":18,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
62,3,1,talk,Benjamin,,"Candidate D, Chronic Care, relies on outside data. The fourteen-month agreement delay makes it risky.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
63,3,1,talk,Yumi,,"Candidate B, Perinatal Support, uses the midwifery slack. That is a strong fit for current staffing.","{""words"":16,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
64,3,1,talk,Servas,,Candidate B is on-site. It bills at the standard rate. It avoids the home visit tariff trap.,"{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
65,3,2,talk,Asuka,,"@Servas, yes. B is on-site. It generates revenue. A does not.","{""words"":11,""mentions"":[""Servas""],""raw_differs"":false}"
66,3,2,talk,Toshio,,"@Benjamin, D is too dependent on external systems. C is contained within the hospital.","{""words"":14,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
67,3,2,talk,Benjamin,,"@Yumi, B is a joint venture between midwifery and pediatrics. We must ensure both hold the budget.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[""Yumi""],""raw_differs"":false}"
68,3,2,talk,Yumi,,"@Benjamin, the midwifery slack is real. Pediatrics is stable. It is a safer joint effort than the failed clinic.","{""words"":19,""mentions"":[""Benjamin""],""raw_differs"":false}"
69,3,2,talk,Servas,,"@Toshio, C requires new equipment and space. The budget is down. B uses existing rooms.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[""Toshio""],""raw_differs"":false}"
70,3,3,talk,Asuka,,"A is clinically valuable, but financially unsound given the tariffs. B is sustainable.","{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
71,3,3,talk,Toshio,,"C is a good idea, but the physical constraints and booking rigidity make it difficult.","{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
72,3,3,talk,Benjamin,,D is too complex. The data agreements are a bottleneck. B is simpler and self-contained.,"{""words"":15,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
73,3,3,talk,Yumi,,B leverages the midwifery capacity. It is the most staffable option right now.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
74,3,3,talk,Servas,,"B fits the budget. It is on-site, billable, and uses existing staff. It is the prudent choice.","{""words"":17,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
75,3,4,talk,Asuka,,I vote for B. It is sustainable and uses existing capacity.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
76,3,4,talk,Toshio,,I vote for B. It fits within the physical and booking constraints.,"{""words"":12,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
77,3,4,talk,Benjamin,,I vote for B. It avoids the data agreement and joint venture pitfalls.,"{""words"":13,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
78,3,4,talk,Yumi,,I vote for B. It utilizes the midwifery slack effectively.,"{""words"":10,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
79,3,4,talk,Servas,,I vote for B. It is financially viable and operationally sound.,"{""words"":11,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
